#{[#edb/ref 17592186049162 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060770} :activity/id #uuid "9b6ebd1c-f083-5c13-ba2f-eefcd05878c6" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060969 :tutorial/content "Consider the region below, which represents the inequality $2 < x < 5$.\n\n![](images/fe/feeae0bfa9b29e6f034a3fd33d1e9fbd369840835af2d6588892ee6edc2fa120.png)\n\nWe can represent this inequality using **interval notation** as follows:\n\n$$\n(2,5)\n$$\n\nThe parentheses $(\\phantom{0})$ tell us that the endpoints $x=2$ and $x=5$ are *not* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval does not include the endpoints, we say that it is **open**.\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (2,5)\n$$\n\nThe symbol $\\in$ means \"lies in\" or \"belongs to.\" So, the above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (open) interval $(2,5)$.*\"" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060782}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059385 :question/id #uuid "9096883e-6dd0-5dd1-8e9c-176775edadba" :question/math-academy-id "e-10785" :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x \\in (1, 5)$?" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only parentheses. So, the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}1, 5{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<}}\\,\\, x \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<}}\\,\\, 5\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Intervals and Inequalities: Open Intervals"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060775}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060970 :tutorial/content "Some intervals may include one parenthesis and one bracket.\n\nFor example, consider the following interval:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{\\big[}}} 1,4 {\\color{red}{\\big)}}\n$$\n\nTo express this interval as an inequality, we note the following:\n\n- The left bracket $\\,\\color{blue}[\\,$ tells us that left endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq}}$ for this endpoint.\n- The right parenthesis $\\,\\color{red})\\,$ tells us that the right endpoint $4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{<}}\\,$ for this endpoint.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq}}\\,\\, x \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,\\, 4\n$$\n\nThe interval can be graphed as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a67872e8193c2bcc249a9a9006acb916909099e4babdbe5cdfa6d2643c10ad2.png)\n\nIntervals with one bracket and one parenthesis are called **half-open** (or **half-closed**)." :tutorial/title "Half-Open Intervals"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060773}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060773 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059386 :question/id #uuid "b828f144-b8b8-5eda-94f1-43cccdcb1aaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10786" :question/problem "Write the interval $y\\in\\big(-4, \\sqrt{2}\\big]$ as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{\\big(}}} -4, \\sqrt2\\, {\\color{blue}{\\big]}}\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,$.\n- The right endpoint $\\sqrt2$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq}}\\,$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is\n\n$$\n-4 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,\\, y \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq}}\\,\\, \\sqrt 2\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Inequalities"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060774}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059387 :question/id #uuid "45d05c7d-e278-552f-a524-926d4a57d870" :question/math-academy-id "e-10787" :question/problem "What interval corresponds to the segment shown below?\n\n![](images/43/430481f9d0863b0bb5602f949e6f994d8007ea29fdd741a485d880df8d5d2dec.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-2\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-2,2{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Number Lines"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059388 :question/id #uuid "6d4bd0a2-0961-54c1-b781-b470d420d848" :question/math-academy-id "e-10816" :question/problem "Express the inequality $1 \\le x \\le4$ in interval notation." :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $1$ and $4$ *are* included.\n\n$$\n1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}1,4{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4]\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Intervals and Inequalities: Closed Intervals"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060782 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060973 :tutorial/content "Now consider the region below, which represents the inequality\n\n$$\n-3 \\leq x \\leq 1\n$$\n\n![](images/38/38c5bf5408bbbeb0f63e198e0564a37e8fe6a34d7f2ce340751d8000443123ed.png)\n\nWe express this inequality using interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-3,1]\n$$\n\nThe brackets $[\\phantom{0}]$ tell us that the endpoints $x=-3$ and $x=1$ *are* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval includes the endpoints, we say that it is **closed**.\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [-3,1]\n$$\n\nThe above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (closed) interval $[-3,1]$.*\"" :tutorial/title "Closed Intervals"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060771}}] :activity/title "Interval Notation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059917}] [#edb/ref 17592186049144 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101708} :activity/id #uuid "415f6847-2cd0-58f9-a597-f65b9de7b7f6" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101973 :tutorial/content "Given the parametric equations of a curve, we can find its Cartesian equation by eliminating the parameter.\n\nFor instance, consider the parametric curve given by\n\n$$\nx=2t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2 \\leq t \\leq 2.\n$$\n\nTo find the Cartesian equation of the curve, we start with the $x$ equation and solve for $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad t=\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nWe now substitute this into our expression for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{8}x^3.$\n\nHowever, our parametric curve was subject to the constraint $-2\\leq t \\leq 2.$ Since $x=2t,$ the corresponding constraint for $x$ is $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{8}x^3, \\quad -4 \\leq x \\leq 4.\n$$\n\nThe graph of this curve is shown below:\n\n![](images/46/4697114d3ce40272a49dff794c4375301ae346e81b823049f587f8780172c413.png)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100889 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100713 :question/id #uuid "cbd0cb3d-cf0f-54db-8cfc-c938e463e4f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5087" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100890 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100714 :question/id #uuid "f3d396ea-caed-51ed-a4d8-0bfc3301f2f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5101" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101974 :tutorial/content "When parametric equations contain trigonometric equations, we can often eliminate the parameter using a trigonometric identity.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cos t, \\,\\,\\, y = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\sin t, \\quad 0 \\leq t\\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nFor the sine and cosine functions, we have the following Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}t\\, & \\Rightarrow \\,\\operatorname{cos}t=2x \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}t\\, & \\Rightarrow \\,\\operatorname{sin}t=3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2}+(3y)^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $4x^2 + 9y^2 = 1.$" :tutorial/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Curves Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101710}}] :activity/title "Cartesian Equations of Parametric Curves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101094}] [#edb/ref 17592186049228 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152992} :activity/id #uuid "f7660d8f-de26-55be-805d-924b98cd9a36" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153478 :tutorial/content "Just as we can find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two monomials, we can also find the least common multiple of two polynomials.\n\nTo find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFor example, let's find the least common multiple of $6(x+3)^2$ and $8(x+3).$\n\nFirst, we find the least common multiple of the constants, $\\textrm{LCM}(6,8)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Multiples of $6 \\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 6, \\, 12, \\, 18, \\, {\\color{blue}24}, \\, 30, \\ldots$\n\n- Multiples of $8 \\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 8, \\, 16, \\, {\\color{blue}24}, \\, 32, \\ldots$\n\nSo, $\\textrm{LCM}(6,8) =  {\\color{blue}{24}}.$\n\nTo find the $\\textrm{LCM}$ of $(x+3)^2$ and $(x+3),$ we raise $(x+3)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x+3)^2, \\, (x+3) \\right) = {\\color{red}{(x+3)^2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(6(x+3)^2, \\, 8(x+3) \\right) = {\\color{blue}{24}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{(x+3)^2}} = 24(x+3)^2.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152993}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152116 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151877 :question/id #uuid "78763507-6493-5a57-91a1-1c0c92324473" :question/math-academy-id "e-14805" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $4(x+1)$ and $6(x+1).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(4,6)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Multiples of $4\\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 4, \\, 8, \\, {\\color{blue}12}, \\ldots$\n\n- Multiples of $6\\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 6, \\, {\\color{blue}12}, \\, 18, \\ldots$\n\nSo, $\\textrm{LCM}(4,6) =  {\\color{blue}{12}}.$\n\nSince the variable part is the same in the expressions, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x+1), \\, (x+1)\\right) = {\\color{red}{(x+1)}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(4(x+1), \\, 6(x+1)\\right) = {\\color{blue}{12}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{(x+1)}} = 12(x+1).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Polynomials that Differ by a Constant Factor"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152117 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151879 :question/id #uuid "588a1460-6cd3-519a-ae98-7bc80c7a946c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14809" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $9(x-1)^4$ and $12(x-1)^2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(9,12)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Multiples of $9 \\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 9, \\, 18, \\, 27, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\, 45, \\ldots$\n\n- Multiples of $12 \\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 12, \\, 24, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\, 48, \\ldots$\n\nSo, $\\textrm{LCM}(9,12) =  {\\color{blue}{36}}.$\n\nTo find the $\\textrm{LCM}$ of $(x-1)^4$ and $(x-1)^2,$ we raise $(x-1)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x-1)^4, \\, (x-1)^2\\right) = {\\color{red}{(x-1)^4}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(9(x-1)^4, \\, 12(x-1)^2\\right) = {\\color{blue}{36}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{(x-1)^4}} = 36(x-1)^4.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152995}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152118 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151880 :question/id #uuid "9a1f928c-ab43-5d93-b187-13889113c46f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14813" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $6(x+3)^2(y+1)^2$ and $9(x+3)(y+1)^4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(6,9)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Multiples of $6\\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 6, \\, 12, \\, {\\color{blue}18}, \\, 24, \\ldots$\n\n- Multiples of $9\\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 9, \\, {\\color{blue}18}, \\, 27, \\ldots$\n\nSo, $\\textrm{LCM}(6,9) =  {\\color{blue}{18}}.$\n\nTo find the $\\textrm{LCM}$ of $(x+3)^2(y+1)^2$ and $(x+3)(y+1)^4,$ we find the largest powers for $(x+3)$ and $(y+1)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For $(x+3)^2$ and $(x+3),$ the largest power is ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n- For $(y+1)^2$ and $(y+1)^4,$ the largest power is ${\\color{red}{4}}.$\n\nNow, we raise $(x+3)$ and $(y+1)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x+3)^2(y+1)^2, \\,(x+3)(y+1)^4\\right) =(x+3)^{\\color{blue}{2}}\\cdot (y+1)^{\\color{red}{4}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(6(x+3)^2(y+1)^2, \\, 9(x+3)(y+1)^4\\right) = 18(x+3)^2 (y+1)^4.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Multivariable Polynomials"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152996}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152119 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151881 :question/id #uuid "3b462bac-92e9-5328-812c-da59d33b07f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14815" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $12(x+2)^4 (y+1),$ $\\,9(x+2) (y+1)^2,$ and $6(x+2)^2 (y+1)^3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(12, 9, 6)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Multiples of $12\\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 12, \\, 24, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\, 48, \\ldots$\n\n- Multiples of $9\\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 9, \\, 18, \\, 27, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\, 45, \\ldots$\n\n- Multiples of $6 \\mathbin{:}$ $\\quad 6, \\, 12, \\, 18, \\, 24, \\, 30, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\ldots$\n\nSo, $\\textrm{LCM}(12, 9, 6) =  {\\color{blue}{36}}.$\n\nTo find the $\\textrm{LCM}$ of $(x+2)^4 (y+1), (x+2) (y+1)^2,$ and $(x+2)^2 (y+1)^3,$ we find the largest powers for $(x+2)$ and $(y+1)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For $(x+2)^4,(x+2),$ and $(x+2)^2,$ the largest power is ${\\color{blue}{4}}.$\n\n- For $(y+1),(y+1)^2,$ and $(y+1)^3,$ the largest power is ${\\color{red}{3}}.$\n\nNow, we raise $(x+2)$ and $(y+1)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x+2)^4 (y+1), \\,(x+2) (y+1)^2,\\,(x+2)^2 (y+1)^3\\right) =(x+2)^{\\color{blue}{4}}\\cdot (y+1)^{\\color{red}{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\text{LCM}(12(x+2)^{4}(y+1),\\,9(x+2)(y+1)^{2},\\,6(x+2)^{2}(y+1)^{3}) \\\\  & =36\\cdot (x+2)^{4}\\cdot (y+1)^{3} \\\\  & =36(x+2)^{4}(y+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Three Polynomials"}}] :activity/title "The Least Common Multiple of Two Polynomials" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152332}] [#edb/ref 17592186049146 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230044} :activity/id #uuid "e1f24137-95a9-5d07-991d-2ce3ba7ada0d" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230177 :tutorial/content "Consider the first-order inhomogeneous ODE with constant coefficients given by\n\n$$\nay' + by = f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nAs we know, the method of finding the general solution is to find the sum of the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ and a particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the particular solution $y_p(x),$ we examine the functional form of the forcing term $f(x)$ and guess a form for $y_p(x)$ that matches it. \n\nAn ODE exhibits **exponential forcing** if the forcing term $f(x)$ is an exponential function\n\n$$\nf(x) = Ke^{k x},\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a constant. \n\nProvided that the forcing function $e^{kx}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, our trial guess for the particular solution is an exponential function with the same exponent. That is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha\\,e^{k x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nIn this lesson, we focus on solving first-order inhomogeneous ODEs with **exponential forcing**. Let's look at a concrete example." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230227 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm{d} y} {\\textrm{d} x} + 3 y = 4 e ^ {2x}.\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm{d} y} {\\textrm{d} x} + 3 y = 0\n$$\n\nand therefore, the complementary solution is \n\n$$\ny_c(x) =  A e^{-3x},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant. Let's now find the **particular solution** $y_p(x)$ to this equation.\n\nSince the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is the exponential function $f(x) = 4 e^{2x},$ and this is not incorporated within the complementary solution, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha e^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\alpha e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation and solve for $\\alpha{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+3(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ (2+3)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ 5\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac{4}{5} e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the **general solution** is given by\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p = A e^{-3x} + \\dfrac{4}{5} e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can employ a similar strategy when the forcing function is a sum of exponential functions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230046}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229162 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228896 :question/id #uuid "5e56989a-feb1-590a-8684-9442dee1b1cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21615" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y =  e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  A e ^ {2x}$, find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x},$ and neither $e^{x}$ nor $e^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{x} + \\beta e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha e^{x} + 3\\beta e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha {e}^{x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x}) & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ (\\alpha -2\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(3\\beta -2\\beta ){e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{x}$ and $e^{3x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-\\alpha = 1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = - e ^{x} - 2 e^{3x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230178 :tutorial/content "So far, our guesses for the particular solution have worked because the forcing term did not already appear in the complementary solution.\n\nSometimes, however, the right-hand side of the differential equation has the same form as a term that appears in the complementary solution $y_c(x).$ When this happens, our usual guess for $y_p(x)$ fails because it duplicates part of the complementary solution and cannot produce a new solution.\n\nIn this situation, we must modify our guess by *multiplying it by $x.$* Specifically, if the forcing term has the form $K e^{kx}$ and the complementary solution contains a term like $Ae^{kx},$ then instead of guessing\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha e^{kx},\n$$\n\nwe try\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\boldsymbol{x} e^{kx}.\n$$\n\nThis works because multiplying by $x$ creates a new function that is *linearly independent* of the complementary solution. As a result, our guess no longer overlaps with $y_c(x)$ and can serve as a valid particular solution. We'll discuss linear independence of functions in more detail in future lessons.\n\nLet’s see how this works in practice using a concrete example." :tutorial/title "Cases When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230229 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 10e^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 0\n$$\n\nand so the complementary solution is \n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNotice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 10 e^{-5x},$ and yet $e^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution. So, in this case, we assume a particular solution that takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha x  e ^ {-5 x} ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of $y_p$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x})^{'} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{'} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation and solve for $\\alpha.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x})+5\\alpha x{e}^{-5x} & =10{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =10{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =10{e}^{-5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 10x  e ^ {-5x}\n$$\n\nand thus the general solution to the equation is\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x) = Ae^{-5x} + 10xe^{-5x}.\n$$" :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229163 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228902 :question/id #uuid "1ea06d66-de30-514a-817f-8749abb4ab25" :question/math-academy-id "e-21621" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 15e^{-5x} + 12e^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  Ae^{-5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15 e^{-5x} + 12 e^{x},$ and $e^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while $e^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {-5 x} + \\beta e^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+5(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+(\\beta +5\\beta ){e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x}+6\\beta {e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{-5x}$ and $e^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 6\\beta = 12,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 15x  e ^ {-5x} + 2 e^{x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229164 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228897 :question/id #uuid "a63b7c7a-2565-5330-8994-54abefbdd191" :question/math-academy-id "e-21616" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 6 y = 9 e ^ {6 x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6 y_c=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9 e ^ {6 x},$ and $e^{6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {6x} ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of $y_p$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{6x}+6x{e}^{6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x})-6\\alpha x{e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ (1+6x-6x)\\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 9x  e ^ {6 x} .\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{6x} + 9xe^{6x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230051}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229165 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228898 :question/id #uuid "d2382def-85b2-5002-9614-692f272355c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21617" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) =  1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' = 3\\alpha e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (3-2)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 4e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) =1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4 & =1 \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -3e^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs With Exponential Forcing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229250}]}
